.TH MAN2SPEAK 1
.SH NAME
man2speak \- a man page as text, structured for reading aloud
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B man2speak
.I file
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Man2speak
parses
.I file
- real troff, the same parser
.IR man2txt (1)
uses,
.B /appl/lib/parseman.b
- and writes it back out as text, differing from
.I man2txt
in exactly one way: a
.B .SH
or
.B .SS
heading is given its own paragraph, a blank line before it and after,
rather than run straight into the body text that follows it.
.PP
.I Man2txt
already knows which text came from a heading - parseman's
.B Text
adt carries a
.B heading
field for exactly that - and throws it away, because a terminal reader
doesn't need the distinction to look right on screen. Piped through
.IR sentences (1),
which only has blank lines to go on, that loss shows: a heading fuses
into the paragraph after it, so
.B "NAME"
and
.B "paste - merge corresponding lines of several files"
come out as one run rather than two. This keeps the one bit of
structure
.I sentences
needs and throws away nothing else, so
.IP
.EX
man2speak /man/1/paste | sentences | speak
.EE
.IP
reads the heading as its own utterance and the body as the sentences it
actually is.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/man2speak.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR man (1),
.IR sentences (1),
.IR speak (1)
.SH BUGS
Understands headings and running text; does not understand troff
tables
.RB ( .TS / .TE )
or equations
.RB ( .EQ / .EN ) ,
because
.I parseman
doesn't parse them either. No man page in this tree uses either; some
.B doc/*.ms
papers do, which is a different, unattempted problem - reading a table
aloud needs its own convention (row and column context per cell, not a
paragraph to reflow), not a fix to this.
